# Extension Invariants

Before changing extension runtime behavior, read the relevant current plan/specification.
The plan is the detailed source of truth; this rule only protects cross-cutting project decisions.

## Permissions and access

- Chrome-managed permissions are the source of truth for site access.
- Do not introduce a separate enabled/disabled-sites authority unless the current specification explicitly changes that design.
- Permission requests/removals that require user consent belong in the user-gesture path.
- Do not broaden extension permissions merely to make implementation easier.

## State

- Keep durable user preference, current tab behavior, and Chrome-managed permission state conceptually separate.
- Do not make storage writes part of the critical playback path.
- Do not let frame- or media-local behavior silently mutate tab-wide or persisted user intent.

## Frames and content scripts

- Preserve the current top-frame/child-frame authority model defined by the plan.
- Do not let a child frame invent tab-wide state.
- Permission removal must reconcile already-running content scripts, not only future registrations.
- Treat opaque/fallback-frame behavior according to the current specification rather than guessing from `location.origin`.

## Media behavior

- Tab-level requested speed and per-video arbitration are separate concerns.
- A single hostile or unusual player must not disable or rewrite state for other videos.
- Preserve one-controller-per-video and lifecycle cleanup guarantees.

## Privacy

- Do not add analytics, telemetry, accounts, remote configuration, remote code,
  or extension-originated network services unless explicitly approved.

## External guidance

- Generic Chrome-extension skills and documentation are advisory.
- Project specifications and repository rules define this extension's intended behavior.
- If platform behavior appears to contradict the current specification, verify the conflict
  and surface it rather than silently redesigning the architecture.